 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Reduction:** Reduces memory consumption by quantizing each grid dimension to a single byte [S1].
-*   **Quality:** Maintains rendering quality comparable to full-precision models when integrated during optimization, avoiding the quality drops seen in post-training quantization [S1].
-*   **Performance:** No significant runtime performance overhead is mentioned, as the quantization is a storage optimization [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires integration into the training pipeline; cannot be easily applied to already-trained models without retraining or fine-tuning [S1].
+*   **Memory Reduction:** Reduces memory consumption by mapping each grid dimension to a single byte (8 bits) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Enables real-time view synthesis in unbounded scenes by reducing render-time memory bandwidth and storage requirements [S1].
+*   **Quality:** Maintains high rendering quality comparable to full-precision models when integrated during training, avoiding the quality drops associated with post-training quantization [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The method is specific to grid-based neural field representations and requires careful selection of the scaling factor $m$ for different attribute types (density vs. color) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in the MERF (Memory-Efficient Radiance Fields) paper for real-time view synthesis in unbounded scenes [S1]. It addresses the high memory consumption of neural field representations by enabling single-byte storage for grid values while preserving rendering quality through differentiable optimization [S1].
+This method was introduced in the MERF (Memory-Efficient Radiance Fields) paper for real-time view synthesis in unbounded scenes [S1]. It addresses the high memory requirements of neural radiance fields by enabling efficient storage and rendering of large-scale scenes [S1].
 
